<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bin Tepe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bin Tepe, Turkish for 'thousand mounds', is a royal cemetery of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. It contains over a hundred burial mounds, including the tumulus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alyattes">Alyattes</a>, more than 350 metres across, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> ranked among the greatest works of man, and a mound often linked with Gyges. Most were robbed in antiquity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bin-tepe.jpg" alt="Bin Tepe" width="320" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Volker Höhfeld · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bintepe_19_04_1984_lydische_K%C3%B6nigsgr%C3%A4ber_Blick_auf_den_Karn%C4%B1yar%C4%B1k_Tepe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
